// Kinyarwanda (rw) — Bantu (JD61, Rwanda-Rundi), Latin orthography, AUTHORED. Referees: epitran
// kin-Latn (programmatic) for the BULK + the Cox "Kirundi & Kinyarwanda Comparative Grammar" (Table 25) for the
// PALATAL series. Wordlist: CC0 Common Voice sentence corpus. → 🔷 single-source.
//
// The palatal analysis was REVISED (from Wikipedia's palatal-segment c/ɟ/ç/bɟ to the grammar's PALATALIZATION
// analysis ⟨Cy⟩→Cʲ: cy=kʲ, jy=ɡʲ, shy=ʃʲ, by=bʲ, ry=ɾʲ, my=mʲ). This is better-sourced (the Cox grammar AND
// epitran both back palatalization) and it RECONCILES with epitran on cy/jy/by/ry/my — those are folded ʲ~j (the
// grammar writes the secondary-articulation ʲ, epitran the full glide j; same palatalized consonant). The residual
// disagreements are exactly ⟨sh⟩ (epitran [xj] vs ours [ʃ]), ⟨c⟩ ([c] vs [t͡ʃ]), ⟨j⟩ ([ɟ] vs [ʒ]) — where the Cox
// grammar AND the coronal-harmony phonetics paper both confirm OURS and epitran is genuinely wrong. So epitran is
// wrong on sh/c/j but RIGHT on cy/jy (an earlier writeup mischaracterised it as wrong on all palatals).
{
    "referees": [
        { "file": "rw.epitran-kin.tsv", "source": "epitran kin-Latn (programmatic)", "role": "primary" }
    ],
    "secondaryGap": "No second machine referee (no wikipron/kaikki for Kinyarwanda). epitran corroborates the bulk + the palatalized ⟨Cy⟩ series (cy/jy/by/ry/my, folded ʲ~j); it is wrong on ⟨sh c j⟩ where the Cox grammar (Table 25) + the coronal-harmony phonetics paper independently confirm ours ([ʃ t͡ʃ ʒ]). The palatal VALUES rest on the Cox grammar (an independent human source), not Wikipedia alone. Wordlist: CC0 Common Voice. → 🔷 single-source.",
    "folds": [
        { "pattern": "ʲ", "replace": "j", "note": "PALATALISATION ⟨Cy⟩→[Cʲ] (Cox grammar Table 25) vs epitran's full glide [Cj] — the same palatalised consonant in two notations; fold ours ʲ→j to compare (we keep ʲ, the secondary articulation, in output). Reconciles cy=kʲ~kj, jy=ɡʲ~ɡj, by=bʲ~bj, ry=ɾʲ~ɾj, my=mʲ~mj." }
    ]
